> Second, English is remarkably flexible.  One can speak it
> with a very bad accent or rearrange the word order and still
> be understood.  But a slight change of inflection in 
> Chinese or a re-arrangement of word order in German, and
> and one draws only blank stares.  English is also powerful
> because it easily absorbs words from other languages.  English
> vocabulary is massive compared to every other language in
> the world.

Most of which is also true of Russian.  Russian nouns have suffixes to 
indicate if they are the subject or object in a sentence, so word order 
can be essentially random; you put the most important thing at the front 
and go on from there.  My Russian teacher claimed that this made Russian 
very well-suited for poetry.

Every language (except French) has absorbed a ton of words in the last 
century.  Everyone the world over uses a 'telefone' and the 'internet'. 
  Wandering around Moscow (and understanding the Cyrillic alphabet), you 
can read most of the signage.  Go into an old church, though, and not a 
word of anything will be comprehensible.


